---
// src/pages/portal/tasks/index.astro — THE BOARD. /portal/tasks
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE OWNS, WHICH IS ALMOST NOTHING. src/lib/employee-tasks.ts already holds the whole
// workflow: the status graph, who may walk which edge, the collaborator roles, the visibility
// clause. src/styles/workforce.css and src/components/workforce/* already hold the whole visual
// language. This page resolves who is asking, hands their users id to listBoard(), groups what comes
// back by TASK_STATUSES, and posts moves back through moveTask(). Every refusal a person reads here
// is a sentence the ENGINE wrote; every colour, every size and every duration is a TOKEN. Two places
// deciding who may finish a task is how one of them becomes the one nobody remembered to update, and
// two places deciding what "overdue" looks like is how a board ends up with three different ambers.
//
// MOVING A CARD WORKS WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT, AND THAT IS THE MECHANISM, NOT THE FALLBACK. Every card
// carries a real <form method="POST"> with a status <select> and a Move button. The drag-and-drop at
// the bottom of this file is one small inline script that does exactly one thing: set that same
// select and submit that same form. If it never runs — old Android WebView, script blocked, a touch
// screen with no HTML5 drag — nothing is lost and nothing is hidden. The script also adds the drag
// affordance itself (`draggable`, the grab cursor), so a browser that cannot drag never shows a
// handle it will not honour. The select is in tab order and is the keyboard path; accessible drag is
// only accessible because the form exists underneath it.
//
// THE SELECT ON EACH CARD IS BUILT FROM t.allowedTransitions AND NOTHING ELSE. That list is computed
// per task, per viewer, from roles the DATABASE resolved in the same query that returned the row. A
// control that exists only to be refused teaches people the system is broken, so a task this person
// cannot move shows no control at all and says so in words instead.
//
// WHY THE ASSIGNEE FILTER IS APPLIED HERE AND THE OTHERS ARE APPLIED IN SQL. listBoard() can filter
// by assignee and is deliberately not asked to. The people in the assignee dropdown are derived from
// the board's own rows — the only list of names this page is entitled to show, since anyone else
// would be a person the viewer cannot see. If the assignee filter ran in SQL, the dropdown would be
// rebuilt from a result set containing exactly one person, and switching from one colleague to
// another would be impossible without first clearing the filter. Priority, overdue and archived stay
// in SQL where they belong. Still ONE query. The cost is that its LIMIT applies before the narrowing,
// so when the board comes back full the page says so rather than presenting a truncation as the whole.
//
// EVERY CARD TITLE LINKS TO /portal/tasks/[id], which exists and is gated the same way this page is:
// getTaskView() decides visibility in its WHERE clause against the viewer's users id, so a card
// nobody may open is a card that was never on this board to click. The link is TaskCard's own `href`,
// which turns the title into a full-width 44px target without covering the move form underneath it.
// The card's <details> still carries the summary (description, the blocked or cancelled reason, who
// assigned it, when it last moved), so the common questions are answered without leaving the board
// at all — the link is for the conversation and the collaborators, which only the detail page has.
//
// =================================================================================================
// ASSIGNING WORK. The disclosure above the board is createTask()'s first and only caller.
// =================================================================================================
//
// WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT ON A PAGE OF ITS OWN. Until this form existed, employee_tasks had no
// reachable INSERT anywhere in the repository: the engine, the ten-state graph, the collaborator
// roles and eleven components were all correct and the table could only ever be empty, so every task
// widget rendered a truthful empty state forever and an intern opening Tasks saw nothing. The board
// is where somebody already is when they think "and X needs to do Y", so the form is a <details>
// shut by default at the top of it rather than a second screen to navigate to. Shut, it costs one
// 44px row; the columns are not pushed down by a form nobody is filling in right now.
//
// WHO MAY BE ASSIGNED TO, AND WHY THE LIST IS THIS SHORT. createTask() authorises three routes and
// re-derives every one of them inside its own INSERT ... SELECT, against the TARGET's row:
//
//     e.user_id = the assigner            -- themselves; self-assigned work is ordinary
//  OR e.reporting_manager_id = the assigner  -- their own reports (this column holds a USERS id)
//  OR e.department_id = scopeDepartmentId    -- present ONLY when the caller passes that scope
//
// and its own doc says `scopeDepartmentId` is the department-head route and must come from
// gate.scopeDepartmentId — which workspace-access.ts populates for role === 'department_head' and
// leaves null for everyone else. So "their own department" here means THE DEPARTMENT THEY LEAD, and
// this page builds its dropdown from exactly those three routes and passes exactly that scope.
//
// The wider reading was considered and rejected: passing the actor's OWN hr_employees.department_id
// as scopeDepartmentId would let any employee put work on any colleague's list, which is a rights
// model this page would be inventing on top of the engine's — and the second copy is always the one
// nobody remembers to update. If assigning across a department without leading it is wanted, it
// belongs in employee-tasks.ts next to the other three routes, not in a dropdown here.
//
// Somebody with no reports and no department to lead therefore sees exactly one option: themselves.
// That is honest — self-assigned work is real work and it puts real rows on their board — and it is
// what the engine will actually accept from them.
//
// THE OPTIONS ARE NOT THE CHECK. The dropdown is drawn from a query, and a query result is a
// suggestion; the POST handler re-asks the database whether this actor may assign to the id that
// arrived, over the same scope fragment, BEFORE createTask() is called — and createTask() then
// re-derives it a third time inside the INSERT itself. A posted assignee outside the scope is
// refused in words and the refusal is audited. That is the same rule as every other write here.
import '@/styles/workforce.css';
import { db } from '@/lib/db';
import { sql, type SQL } from 'drizzle-orm';
import { logAudit } from '@/lib/audit';
import {
  requireEmployee,
  requireTeamLead,
  DEPARTMENT_COVERAGE_NOTICE,
} from '@/lib/auth/workspace-access';
import {
  TASK_STATUSES,
  STATUS_LABELS,
  TASK_PRIORITIES,
  canonicalStatus,
  isTaskStatus,
  listBoard,
  moveTask,
  createTask,
  ensureTaskSchema,
  type TaskStatus,
  type TaskPriority,
  type BoardTask,
} from '@/lib/employee-tasks';
import WorkspaceNav from '@/components/workforce/WorkspaceNav.astro';
import FilterBar from '@/components/workforce/FilterBar.astro';
import TaskCard from '@/components/workforce/TaskCard.astro';
import EmptyState from '@/components/workforce/EmptyState.astro';

const user = Astro.locals.user as any;

// postgres-js resolves to a plain array, never a { rows } object. Declared before everything that
// uses it: `const` is not hoisted, and a handler reaching a later declaration has taken pages down
// in this repo before.
const rowsOf = (r: any): any[] => (Array.isArray(r) ? r : (r?.rows || []));

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GATE FIRST. Nobody unresolvable sees a board.
//
// requireEmployee() is the base gate for a personal work surface and is the right one here: the
// board is somebody's own work. It does insist on an active hr_employees row, and there is exactly
// one person that wrongly excludes — a department head whose own HR record HR has not created yet.
// requireTeamLead() exists precisely for that case (its doc comment: authority is
// users.role + users.assigned_department_id, and demanding an employee row would lock a real lead out
// without narrowing what they may see by a single row), so a 'no-employee-record' denial gets a
// second look through that gate and nothing else does. 'ambiguous-record' must NOT fall through —
// that denial is the deliberate refusal to guess between two people — and neither must 'inactive'
// (a closed record stays closed) nor 'lookup-failed' (the database blinked; guessing again is not an
// answer). Either way the CONTENTS of the board are scoped by listBoard()'s own WHERE clause against
// this users id, so the gate decides whether a person is resolvable, never what they may read.
const gate = await requireEmployee(user, '/portal/tasks');
const leadGate = (!gate.ok && gate.code === 'no-employee-record')
  ? await requireTeamLead(user, '/portal/tasks')
  : null;

const denial = gate.ok ? null : (leadGate && leadGate.ok ? null : gate);
if (denial && denial.redirect) return Astro.redirect(denial.redirect);

const mayUse = !denial;
const viewerUserId = String(user?.id || '').trim();
const myEmployeeId = gate.ok
  ? gate.workspace.employeeId
  : (leadGate && leadGate.ok && leadGate.workspace ? leadGate.workspace.employeeId : null);
const viewerName = String(user?.name || user?.email || '').trim();

// The department a lead may assign into, FROM THE SESSION and nowhere else. workspace-access.ts
// populates it for role === 'department_head' only and leaves it null for everyone else, so the
// department route below simply is not in the statement for anybody else — there is no ?department=
// to try and no wider scope a form field can ask for.
const scopeDepartmentId = gate.ok
  ? gate.scopeDepartmentId
  : (leadGate && leadGate.ok ? leadGate.scopeDepartmentId : null);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EVERY const BELOW IS DECLARED ABOVE THE POST HANDLER. `const` is not hoisted; a handler that
// reaches a declaration further down throws on its first line while the page still reports success.
// That has taken this site down twice. Nothing in the POST block reads anything declared after it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const params = Astro.url.searchParams;

/** Assignee narrowing. Validated against the board's own people list after the read, not here. */
const who = String(params.get('who') || '').trim().slice(0, 64);

const rawPriority = String(params.get('prio') || '').trim().toLowerCase();
const priority: TaskPriority | null =
  (TASK_PRIORITIES as string[]).indexOf(rawPriority) >= 0 ? (rawPriority as TaskPriority) : null;
// An unusable filter is dropped AND said out loud. A full board under a chip still reading "Urgent"
// is the page lying about what it is showing.
const priorityNotice = rawPriority && !priority
  ? 'That priority is not one we track, so the board is not filtered by priority.'
  : '';

const overdueOnly = params.get('late') === '1';
const includeArchived = params.get('arch') === '1';

const rawColumn = String(params.get('col') || '').trim().toLowerCase();
const oneColumn: TaskStatus | null = isTaskStatus(rawColumn) ? rawColumn : null;
const columnNotice = rawColumn && !oneColumn
  ? 'That is not a column we have, so the whole board is shown.'
  : '';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DENSITY AND FOCUS MODE. A cookie, a data attribute on <html>, and not one line of JavaScript.
//
// Both are read here and WRITTEN by the POST handler below, because a display preference that lives
// in the URL is a preference somebody accidentally shares in a link, and a preference that lives in
// localStorage is one that arrives after first paint and reflows the board underneath the reader.
// A cookie is on the request, so the very first byte of HTML is already in the right density.
//
// The values are validated against these lists before they reach an attribute. Neither is ever
// interpolated raw: 'compact' is a value this file recognises or it is 'cozy'.
const DENSITIES = ['comfortable', 'cozy', 'compact'];
const DENSITY_LABELS = ['Comfortable', 'Cozy', 'Compact'];
const rawDensity = String(Astro.cookies.get('wf_density')?.value || '').trim().toLowerCase();
const density = DENSITIES.indexOf(rawDensity) >= 0 ? rawDensity : 'cozy';
const focusOn = String(Astro.cookies.get('wf_focus')?.value || '').trim().toLowerCase() === 'on';

// A year, path-wide, and httpOnly because nothing on the client ever reads it — the server stamps
// the attribute. `secure` is conditional so the cookie is not silently dropped on a local http run.
const COOKIE_OPTS = {
  path: '/',
  maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 365,
  sameSite: 'lax' as const,
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: Astro.url.protocol === 'https:',
};

/**
 * Build a URL for this page carrying the CURRENT filters, with named overrides.
 *
 * Every link and both forms go through this, so a move, a column jump or a filter change never
 * silently drops the other filters — coming back to a board that has quietly reset itself is how
 * somebody loses the view they were working in. Density and focus are NOT in here: they are cookies,
 * so they survive every one of these navigations without occupying a query parameter.
 */
const boardUrl = (over: any = {}): string => {
  const has = (k: string) => Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(over, k);
  const p = new URLSearchParams();

  const nextWho = has('who') ? over.who : who;
  if (nextWho) p.set('who', String(nextWho).slice(0, 64));

  const nextPrio = has('prio') ? over.prio : priority;
  if (nextPrio) p.set('prio', String(nextPrio));

  const nextLate = has('late') ? over.late : overdueOnly;
  if (nextLate) p.set('late', '1');

  const nextArch = has('arch') ? over.arch : includeArchived;
  if (nextArch) p.set('arch', '1');

  const nextCol = has('col') ? over.col : oneColumn;
  if (nextCol) p.set('col', String(nextCol));

  if (over.msg) p.set('msg', String(over.msg).slice(0, 300));
  if (over.bad) p.set('bad', '1');
  // The task that was just created, so the confirmation can offer to open it. Only ever set by the
  // assign redirect and never carried onward by a filter change — it describes one moment.
  if (over.made) p.set('made', String(over.made).slice(0, 64));

  const s = p.toString();
  return s ? '/portal/tasks?' + s : '/portal/tasks';
};

/** Declared before the handler that uses it, and before the JSX that uses it. */
const selfUrl = boardUrl({});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHO THIS PERSON MAY PUT WORK ON. One fragment, used by the dropdown AND by the handler.
//
// It is a single definition on purpose. A page that lists people with one query and then checks a
// submission with a differently-worded second one has two rights models, and the day they drift the
// narrower one is the dropdown — which is the half nobody tests, because the wrong option is simply
// never clicked. Here the SELECT that draws the options and the SELECT that authorises the POST are
// the same text with a different projection.
//
// It mirrors createTask()'s own WHERE clause, and deliberately cannot be wider than it: the lead
// route appears only when the SESSION carries a department scope, which is exactly the value passed
// to createTask() as `scopeDepartmentId`. Every comparison is ::text — reporting_manager_id holds a
// USERS id, department_id is a varchar(50) slug in src/lib/db/schema.ts and a UUID in
// db/hr-schema.sql, and a ::uuid cast would throw on half the estate rather than return no rows.
//
// An unresolvable viewer compiles to `false`: nobody, never everybody.
const assignLeadClause: SQL = scopeDepartmentId
  ? sql`OR e.department_id::text = ${scopeDepartmentId}`
  : sql``;

const assignableScope: SQL = viewerUserId
  ? sql`(e.user_id::text = ${viewerUserId}
      OR e.reporting_manager_id::text = ${viewerUserId}
      ${assignLeadClause})`
  : sql`false`;

/**
 * May this actor put work on this employee, and what is that person called?
 *
 * Returns null when the QUESTION COULD NOT BE ASKED — the column is missing, the database blinked —
 * which is a different fact from "no", and the handler says a different sentence for it. Treating a
 * failed check as a refusal would be tolerable; treating it as permission would not, and returning
 * a bare boolean is how the second one eventually happens.
 *
 * ensureTaskSchema() first because this query NAMES reporting_manager_id, which is ALTERed in by
 * that ensure block and by two admin pages, and a query that names an absent column throws where a
 * SELECT * would not. That is the work_email failure exactly.
 */
const checkAssignable = async (employeeId: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean; name: string } | null> => {
  const id = String(employeeId || '').trim();
  if (!id || !viewerUserId) return { ok: false, name: '' };
  try {
    await ensureTaskSchema();
    const r = rowsOf(await db.execute(sql`
      SELECT e.full_name
        FROM hr_employees e
       WHERE e.id::text = ${id}
         AND e.is_active = true
         AND ${assignableScope}
       LIMIT 1`))[0];
    if (!r) return { ok: false, name: '' };
    return { ok: true, name: String(r.full_name || '').trim() };
  } catch (e: any) {
    // Never a bare catch. The real Postgres reason is on e.cause; e.message is only the SQL.
    console.error('[portal/tasks] assignable check', e?.cause?.message || e?.message);
    return null;
  }
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE ASSIGN FORM IS HOLDING. Declared above the handler that writes them and above the JSX
// that reads them.
//
// A REFUSED ASSIGNMENT RE-RENDERS; IT DOES NOT REDIRECT. Every other write on this page is
// POST-redirect-GET, which is right for a move (two fields, both re-choosable in a second) and
// wrong for a form somebody has just typed four thousand characters of brief into: the query string
// cannot carry it, so a redirect would throw their words away to protect them from a double-submit
// that a refused create cannot cause — nothing was written. SUCCESS still redirects, which is what
// makes a refresh unable to create the task twice.
let assignError = '';
let assignOpen = false;
let assignTitle = '';
let assignDescription = '';
let assignWho = '';
let assignPriority = 'normal';
let assignDue = '';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE WRITES. Three actions: assign a task, move a task, or change a display preference. All three
// POST to this page. Two of them redirect; the assign form redirects on SUCCESS and re-renders on a
// refusal, for the reason set out above assignError.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if (Astro.request.method === 'POST') {
  // The gate runs above this block, so an unresolvable person cannot post a write even by hand.
  // The engine would refuse them anyway — moveTask() re-derives the role inside the UPDATE, and
  // createTask() re-derives the right to assign inside the INSERT — but a write must never be
  // attempted from a page state that was refused.
  if (!mayUse) {
    return Astro.redirect(boardUrl({ msg: 'Please sign in again to assign or move tasks.', bad: 1 }));
  }

  let msg = '';
  let bad = 1;
  // Set only by the assign branch, and only when nothing was written. It suppresses the redirect at
  // the end of this block so the page renders with the form still filled in and the reason showing.
  let renderInstead = false;

  try {
    const fd = await Astro.request.formData();
    const action = String(fd.get('action') || '').trim();

    // ---- display preference -------------------------------------------------------------
    // A POST rather than a link, because it changes stored state; a redirect afterwards, so a
    // reload does not re-submit it. Only values on the allow-lists are ever written.
    if (action === 'prefs') {
      const wantedDensity = String(fd.get('density') || '').trim().toLowerCase();
      const wantedFocus = String(fd.get('focus') || '').trim().toLowerCase();
      if (DENSITIES.indexOf(wantedDensity) >= 0) {
        Astro.cookies.set('wf_density', wantedDensity, COOKIE_OPTS);
      }
      if (wantedFocus === 'on' || wantedFocus === 'off') {
        Astro.cookies.set('wf_focus', wantedFocus, COOKIE_OPTS);
      }
      return Astro.redirect(selfUrl);
    }

    // ---- assign ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The one caller createTask() has. Everything typed is echoed back into the form on a refusal,
    // so nothing here throws somebody's brief away to report a missing title.
    if (action === 'assign') {
      assignTitle = String(fd.get('title') || '').trim().slice(0, 300);
      assignDescription = String(fd.get('description') || '').trim().slice(0, 4000);
      assignWho = String(fd.get('assignee') || '').trim().slice(0, 64);
      assignDue = String(fd.get('due') || '').trim().slice(0, 20);
      const wantedPrio = String(fd.get('priority') || '').trim().toLowerCase();
      assignPriority = (TASK_PRIORITIES as string[]).indexOf(wantedPrio) >= 0 ? wantedPrio : 'normal';
      assignOpen = true;

      if (assignTitle.length < 3) {
        // The engine refuses this too. It is checked here as well so the person is told before a
        // round trip, not instead of one.
        assignError = 'Give the task a title of at least three characters. It is the only thing the person picking this up will see first.';
        renderInstead = true;
      } else if (!assignWho) {
        assignError = 'Choose who the task is for.';
        renderInstead = true;
      } else if (assignDue && !/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/.test(assignDue)) {
        assignError = 'Give the due date as YYYY-MM-DD, or leave it empty.';
        renderInstead = true;
      } else if (wantedPrio && (TASK_PRIORITIES as string[]).indexOf(wantedPrio) < 0) {
        assignError = 'That is not a priority we track. Pick one of the four.';
        renderInstead = true;
      } else {
        // SERVER-SIDE AUTHORITY. The options this form drew are not the check: they were drawn from
        // a query whose result is now several seconds old, on a page that may have been open since
        // this morning, and the id in the body is whatever the browser sent. Ask the database again,
        // over the same scope fragment, before anything is written.
        const target = await checkAssignable(assignWho);

        if (target === null) {
          // The check did not run. Refuse and say so — an unanswered authority question is not a yes.
          assignError = 'We could not confirm who you may assign work to just now, so nothing was created. Nothing has been lost. Try again in a moment.';
          renderInstead = true;
        } else if (!target.ok) {
          // One sentence whether the record does not exist, is closed, or is simply not theirs to
          // assign to. Probing ids must not be able to tell those apart.
          assignError = 'You cannot assign work to that person. You can assign to yourself, to anyone HR has recorded as reporting to you, and to your department if you lead one.';
          renderInstead = true;
          // A posted assignee outside the scope is worth a record. It is not proof of anything on
          // its own — a stale page reaches here honestly — but a change to who somebody can put
          // work on is an authority question, and the refusals are the half that is never visible
          // otherwise. No entityId: nothing was created, so there is no task to hang it on.
          await logAudit({
            userId: viewerUserId,
            action: 'task.assign.refused',
            entity: 'employee_task',
            diff: { employeeId: assignWho, reason: 'out-of-scope' },
            ipAddress: Astro.clientAddress,
          });
        } else {
          // The engine re-derives this authority a THIRD time, inside its own INSERT ... SELECT, and
          // audits the assignment itself through src/lib/audit.ts (action 'task.assign', with this
          // ip). It is not logged again here: two audit rows for one assignment would be a lie about
          // what happened.
          const res = await createTask({
            employeeId: assignWho,
            assignedByUserId: viewerUserId,
            title: assignTitle,
            description: assignDescription || null,
            priority: assignPriority as TaskPriority,
            dueOn: assignDue || null,
            // The department-head route, from the session. Null for everyone else, and then the
            // clause is not in createTask's statement at all.
            scopeDepartmentId,
            status: 'assigned',
            ipAddress: Astro.clientAddress,
          });

          if (res.ok) {
            // REDIRECT ON SUCCESS, so a refresh re-loads the board instead of creating the task a
            // second time. `made` carries the new id for the confirmation link and is dropped by the
            // next navigation.
            const named = target.name || 'them';
            return Astro.redirect(boardUrl({
              msg: 'Assigned to ' + named + '.',
              bad: 0,
              made: res.id || '',
            }));
          }

          assignError = res.error || 'Could not create that task.';
          renderInstead = true;
        }
      }
    }

    // ---- move ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // An `else if`, not a fall-through: a refused assignment must not then be read as a move with no
    // task_id and reported as "that task is not available", which is a sentence about a task nobody
    // touched. Anything that is neither is an action this page does not have, and nothing was
    // changed, so nothing is said.
    if (action === 'move') {
      const taskId = String(fd.get('task_id') || '').trim();
      const rawTo = String(fd.get('to') || '').trim();
      const reason = String(fd.get('reason') || '').trim();
      // canonicalStatus() rather than a list restated here: one vocabulary, owned by the engine.
      const to = canonicalStatus(rawTo);

      if (!taskId) {
        msg = 'That task is not available.';
      } else if (!rawTo) {
        // Distinct from an unknown status. Somebody who pressed Move without choosing needs telling
        // what to do, not telling that they typed something invalid.
        msg = 'Pick where to move it, then press Move.';
      } else if (!to) {
        msg = 'That is not a status we track.';
      } else {
        // The engine validates the edge, the role and the required reason, and re-derives the
        // authorising role inside the UPDATE. This page adds no checks of its own and overrides none.
        const res = await moveTask(taskId, viewerUserId, to, {
          reason,
          ipAddress: Astro.clientAddress,
        });
        if (res.ok) {
          bad = 0;
          msg = res.changed === false
            ? 'That task was already there, so nothing changed.'
            : 'Moved to ' + (STATUS_LABELS[to] || to) + '.';
        } else {
          msg = res.error || 'Could not move that task.';
        }
      }
    } else if (!renderInstead) {
      return Astro.redirect(selfUrl);
    }
  } catch (e: any) {
    // Never a bare catch. The real Postgres reason lives on e.cause; e.message is only the SQL.
    console.error('[portal/tasks] post', e?.cause?.message || e?.message);
    msg = 'Could not do that just now. Nothing was changed. Try again in a moment.';
    // An exception is not a refusal the form can explain, so it is reported the ordinary way rather
    // than left sitting under the assign disclosure.
    renderInstead = false;
  }

  // The assign branch renders instead of redirecting so nothing typed is thrown away. Everything
  // else on this page is POST-redirect-GET.
  if (!renderInstead) return Astro.redirect(boardUrl({ msg, bad: bad ? 1 : 0 }));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE READ. One query.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// 600 rather than the engine's default 400, because this board is shared by a lead's whole
// department and the assignee narrowing happens after the LIMIT (see the header note). Truncation is
// detected below and stated rather than hidden.
const BOARD_LIMIT = 600;

let boardOk = false;
let boardReason = 'no-viewer';
let allTasks: BoardTask[] = [];
let engineNotice = '';

if (mayUse) {
  try {
    const view = await listBoard(viewerUserId, {
      priority,
      overdueOnly,
      includeArchived,
      limit: BOARD_LIMIT,
    });
    boardOk = view.ok;
    boardReason = view.reason;
    allTasks = Array.isArray(view.tasks) ? view.tasks : [];
    engineNotice = view.notice || '';
  } catch (e: any) {
    // listBoard catches its own failures and reports ok:false, so this only fires on something it
    // could not. It still logs, because a page that renders an empty board on a swallowed error is
    // telling a lead their department has no work in flight.
    console.error('[portal/tasks] listBoard', e?.cause?.message || e?.message);
    boardOk = false;
    boardReason = 'lookup-failed';
  }
}

const truncated = boardOk && allTasks.length >= BOARD_LIMIT;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ASSIGN ROSTER. The same scope fragment the handler authorises with, projected for a dropdown.
//
// It is read whether or not the board read succeeded: being unable to see the board is no reason to
// be unable to give somebody a job, and the two failures are independent.
//
// Three columns, and not one more. The portal's older employee lookups do SELECT *, which drags
// gender, Aadhaar, PAN and bank details into the render context of a page that shows none of them —
// `gender` is the exact column read in the 2026-08-02 breach. A picker needs an id, a name, and
// enough to tell two people with the same name apart.
const ASSIGN_ROSTER_LIMIT = 200;

let assignRosterOk = false;
let assignRosterTruncated = false;
const assignPeople: any[] = [];

if (mayUse) {
  try {
    // NAMES reporting_manager_id, so the ensure block that ALTERs it in runs first. See
    // checkAssignable() for why a query that names an absent column is a different animal from one
    // that does not.
    await ensureTaskSchema();
    const roster = rowsOf(await db.execute(sql`
      SELECT e.id::text AS id,
             e.full_name,
             e.designation,
             (e.user_id::text = ${viewerUserId}) AS is_me,
             (e.reporting_manager_id::text = ${viewerUserId}) AS is_report
        FROM hr_employees e
       WHERE e.is_active = true
         AND ${assignableScope}
       ORDER BY e.full_name ASC
       LIMIT ${ASSIGN_ROSTER_LIMIT}`));

    assignRosterOk = true;
    assignRosterTruncated = roster.length >= ASSIGN_ROSTER_LIMIT;

    for (const r of roster) {
      const id = String(r.id || '').trim();
      if (!id) continue;
      assignPeople.push({
        id,
        // A name is not a permission and its absence is not an error. Never render a bare UUID.
        name: String(r.full_name || '').trim() || 'Name not recorded',
        designation: String(r.designation || '').trim(),
        // is_me and is_report come back NULL when the underlying column is NULL, so both are tested
        // for an explicit true. The order matters: somebody can be both your report and in the
        // department you lead, and they are listed once, under the closer relationship.
        relation: r.is_me === true ? 'me' : (r.is_report === true ? 'report' : 'dept'),
      });
    }
  } catch (e: any) {
    // The form is a convenience; losing it must not lose the board. It fails CLOSED and says so
    // rather than drawing an empty dropdown that reads as "there is nobody you can assign to".
    console.error('[portal/tasks] assign roster', e?.cause?.message || e?.message);
    assignRosterOk = false;
  }
}

// Grouped for the dropdown's optgroups, so the list says WHY each person is on it. Plain arrays
// filtered here rather than a map indexed inside the template — a Record<string,string> read from
// JSX is a known parser trap in .astro files here.
const assignMine = assignPeople.filter((p: any) => p.relation === 'me');
const assignReports = assignPeople.filter((p: any) => p.relation === 'report');
const assignDept = assignPeople.filter((p: any) => p.relation === 'dept');

/** Name, plus the designation when there is one — two colleagues can share a name. */
const personLabel = (p: any): string => (p.designation ? p.name + ', ' + p.designation : p.name);

const assignPrioOptions = (TASK_PRIORITIES as string[]).map((p) => ({
  value: p,
  label: p.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + p.slice(1),
}));

// The people on this board — derived from its own rows, so this dropdown can never name somebody the
// viewer is not already entitled to see.
const people: any[] = [];
const seenPerson: any = {};
for (const t of allTasks) {
  const id = String(t.employeeId || '');
  if (!id || seenPerson[id]) continue;
  seenPerson[id] = 1;
  people.push({
    id,
    name: String(t.employeeName || '').trim() || 'Name not recorded',
    isMe: !!myEmployeeId && id === myEmployeeId,
  });
}
people.sort((a: any, b: any) => String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name)));

const whoValid = who && people.some((p: any) => p.id === who) ? who : null;
const whoNotice = who && !whoValid
  ? 'Nobody with work on this board matches that person, so the board is not filtered by assignee.'
  : '';
const whoName = whoValid ? (people.find((p: any) => p.id === whoValid)?.name || '') : '';

const shown: BoardTask[] = whoValid ? allTasks.filter((t) => String(t.employeeId) === whoValid) : allTasks;

// Columns from TASK_STATUSES, in its order. `archived` is only a column when it was asked for —
// otherwise it is an empty box on every board forever.
const statusesShown: TaskStatus[] = TASK_STATUSES.filter((s) => includeArchived || s !== 'archived');
const columns = statusesShown.map((status) => ({
  status,
  label: STATUS_LABELS[status] || status,
  tasks: shown.filter((t) => t.status === status),
}));
const visibleColumns = oneColumn ? columns.filter((c) => c.status === oneColumn) : columns;

const totalShown = shown.length;
const overdueShown = shown.filter((t) => t.isOverdue).length;

const filtersOn = !!(whoValid || priority || overdueOnly || includeArchived || oneColumn);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Display helpers. Functions rather than typed maps indexed inside JSX — a Record<string,string>
// read from a template is a known parser trap in .astro files here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const labelOf = (s: string): string => STATUS_LABELS[s as TaskStatus] || s;

/** A real sentence per column, so an empty column says what belongs in it. */
const emptyTitle = (s: string): string => {
  if (s === 'draft') return 'Nothing in draft';
  if (s === 'assigned') return 'Nothing waiting to be picked up';
  if (s === 'accepted') return 'Nothing accepted and not started';
  if (s === 'in_progress') return 'Nothing in flight';
  if (s === 'blocked') return 'Nothing is blocked';
  if (s === 'under_review') return 'Nothing waiting to be read';
  if (s === 'approved') return 'Nothing signed off and open';
  if (s === 'completed') return 'Nothing finished yet';
  if (s === 'cancelled') return 'Nothing cancelled';
  if (s === 'archived') return 'Nothing archived';
  return 'Nothing here';
};

// An empty Blocked column is the best news on the board and is drawn as such: the tick, on the
// approved ramp. Everything else gets the calm empty tray.
const emptyArt = (s: string): 'check' | 'tray' => (s === 'blocked' ? 'check' : 'tray');
const emptyTone = (s: string): string => (s === 'blocked' ? 'approved' : 'draft');

/** Only worth a text box when one of the two statuses that demand a stated cause is reachable. */
const needsReasonBox = (t: BoardTask): boolean =>
  t.allowedTransitions.indexOf('blocked') >= 0 || t.allowedTransitions.indexOf('cancelled') >= 0;

// Options for the filter bar. Built as plain {value,label} pairs — no literal-type narrowing to go
// wrong — and passed into a component that renders a GET form and no JavaScript at all.
const whoOptions = [{ value: '', label: 'Everyone' }].concat(
  people.map((p: any) => ({ value: String(p.id), label: p.isMe ? p.name + ' (you)' : p.name })),
);
const prioOptions = [{ value: '', label: 'Any priority' }].concat(
  (TASK_PRIORITIES as string[]).map((p) => ({ value: p, label: p.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + p.slice(1) })),
);

const flash = String(params.get('msg') || '').slice(0, 300);
const flashBad = params.get('bad') === '1';

// The task the assign form just created, if this render followed that redirect. Validated as a UUID
// before it is allowed anywhere near an href: it arrives in a query string, and a link built from
// unvalidated request text is a link somebody else can choose the destination of.
const rawMade = String(params.get('made') || '').trim();
const madeId = /^[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}$/.test(rawMade)
  ? rawMade : '';

const filterSummary = totalShown + (totalShown === 1 ? ' task' : ' tasks')
  + (overdueShown > 0 ? ', ' + overdueShown + ' past their date' : '');

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE COMPOSITION, FOR THE NAVIGATION ONLY.
//
// Last await of the frontmatter, below the POST handler, which reads nothing from it — and every
// write on this page ends in a redirect through boardUrl(), so moving or assigning a task costs no
// composition at all. It never throws out of here: a bar that cannot be built renders no bar, and
// the board itself is untouched.
//
// The gate above is requireEmployee-or-requireTeamLead; this is composeWorkspace, which asks the
// same requireEmployee and then resolves permissions and relationships on top. It decides nothing
// about the BOARD — listBoard()'s own WHERE clause does that, against this users id — and is used
// here for one thing: which doors this person may be offered.
let composed: any = null;
try {
  const { composeWorkspace } = await import('@/lib/workforce/composer');
  composed = await composeWorkspace(user as any, { locals: Astro.locals, next: '/portal/tasks' });
} catch (e: any) {
  console.error('[portal/tasks] compose', e?.cause?.message || e?.message);
}
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-density={density} data-focus-mode={focusOn ? 'on' : 'off'}>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover"/>
  <title>Task board - EduRankAI</title>
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex"/>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg"/>
</head>
<body>

<a class="skip wf-focusable" href="#board">Skip to the board</a>

<header class="bar">
  <a class="bar__back wf-focusable" href="/portal/employee" aria-label="Back to My HR">
    <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="15 18 9 12 15 6"/></svg>
  </a>
  <h1 class="bar__title">Task board</h1>
  {viewerName && <span class="bar__who">{viewerName}</span>}
</header>

<main class="page">

  {/* WHETHER THE MOVE WORKED IS NOT ALLOWED TO BE A COLOUR. The green ramp and the red ramp are
      matched for contrast, which is precisely what makes them one grey in greyscale and one colour
      under deuteranopia — the reason StatusBadge draws ten shapes rather than ten hues. So the
      outcome carries a glyph too, and a word for the screen reader that sees neither. */}
  {flash && (
    <p class="note note--flash" role="status" data-wf-tone={flashBad ? 'danger' : 'approved'}>
      <svg class="note__glyph" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/>
        {flashBad
          ? <><path d="M12 7.4v5.4"/><path d="M12 16.4h.01"/></>
          : <path d="m8.2 12.3 2.6 2.6 5-5.4"/>}
      </svg>
      <span class="wf-sr-only">{flashBad ? 'Not done. ' : 'Done. '}</span>
      <span class="note__text">
        {flash}
        {madeId && <a class="link wf-focusable note__link" href={'/portal/tasks/' + madeId}>Open the task</a>}
      </span>
    </p>
  )}

  {denial && (
    <section class="panel">
      <h2 class="panel__title">{denial.title}</h2>
      <p class="panel__body">{denial.reason}</p>
      <p class="panel__body"><a class="link wf-focusable" href="/portal/employee">Go to My HR</a></p>
    </section>
  )}

  {mayUse && !boardOk && (
    <p class="note" role="status" data-wf-tone="danger">
      {boardReason === 'no-viewer'
        ? 'We could not work out who is asking, so no board is shown. Sign in again and reload this page.'
        : 'The board could not be loaded just now, so this is not a list of your work. Nothing has been lost. Reload in a moment, and tell HR if it keeps happening.'}
    </p>
  )}

  {/* ASSIGNING WORK. Shut by default, so it costs one row of the board rather than a column of it,
      and OUTSIDE the boardOk branch on purpose: a board that could not be read is no reason to stop
      somebody handing out a job. It re-opens by itself when a submission was refused, with what they
      typed still in the fields — see the note on renderInstead above. No script anywhere in it: a
      native disclosure, a real form posting to this page, and nothing else. */}
  {mayUse && (
    <details class="asn" open={assignOpen}>
      <summary class="asn__summary wf-focusable">
        <svg class="asn__plus" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M12 5v14"/><path d="M5 12h14"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="asn__summarytext">Assign a task</span>
        <svg class="asn__caret" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="m7 10 5 5 5-5"/>
        </svg>
      </summary>

      <div class="asn__panel">
        {assignError && (
          <p class="note asn__err" role="alert" data-wf-tone="danger">
            <span class="wf-sr-only">Not done. </span>{assignError}
          </p>
        )}

        {!assignRosterOk && (
          <p class="note" data-wf-tone="danger">
            We could not work out who you can give work to just now, so this form is not shown and
            nothing has been lost. Reload in a moment, and tell HR if it keeps happening.
          </p>
        )}

        {assignRosterOk && assignPeople.length === 0 && (
          <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">
            There is nobody here to choose from, not even yourself. That happens when no active
            employee record is linked to this sign-in. Ask HR to link your employee record to the
            address you sign in with, then reload this page.
          </p>
        )}

        {assignRosterOk && assignPeople.length > 0 && (
          <form class="asn__form" method="POST" action={selfUrl}>
            <input type="hidden" name="action" value="assign"/>

            <div class="asn__field">
              <label class="asn__label" for="asn-title">What needs doing</label>
              <input
                class="asn__input"
                id="asn-title"
                type="text"
                name="title"
                value={assignTitle}
                required
                minlength="3"
                maxlength="300"
                autocomplete="off"
                placeholder="Write the outcome, not the activity"
              />
            </div>

            <div class="asn__field">
              <label class="asn__label" for="asn-who">Who it is for</label>
              {/* Built from the roster query and nothing else, and grouped so the list says why each
                  person is on it. A control that exists only to be refused teaches people the system
                  is broken — and the handler re-asks the database anyway. */}
              <select class="asn__input" id="asn-who" name="assignee" required>
                <option value="">Choose a person</option>
                {assignMine.length > 0 && (
                  <optgroup label="You">
                    {assignMine.map((p: any) => (
                      <option value={p.id} selected={assignWho === p.id}>{p.name + ' (you)'}</option>
                    ))}
                  </optgroup>
                )}
                {assignReports.length > 0 && (
                  <optgroup label="Reports to you">
                    {assignReports.map((p: any) => (
                      <option value={p.id} selected={assignWho === p.id}>{personLabel(p)}</option>
                    ))}
                  </optgroup>
                )}
                {assignDept.length > 0 && (
                  <optgroup label="The department you lead">
                    {assignDept.map((p: any) => (
                      <option value={p.id} selected={assignWho === p.id}>{personLabel(p)}</option>
                    ))}
                  </optgroup>
                )}
              </select>
            </div>

            <div class="asn__pair">
              <div class="asn__field">
                <label class="asn__label" for="asn-prio">Priority</label>
                <select class="asn__input" id="asn-prio" name="priority">
                  {assignPrioOptions.map((o: any) => (
                    <option value={o.value} selected={assignPriority === o.value}>{o.label}</option>
                  ))}
                </select>
              </div>

              <div class="asn__field">
                <label class="asn__label" for="asn-due">Due date</label>
                {/* type="date" on a phone, a plain text box on anything that does not know it — the
                    pattern is there for exactly that fallback, and the handler validates the shape
                    again regardless. Optional: a date nobody agreed is worse than no date. */}
                <input
                  class="asn__input"
                  id="asn-due"
                  type="date"
                  name="due"
                  value={assignDue}
                  pattern="[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}"
                  placeholder="YYYY-MM-DD"
                />
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="asn__field">
              <label class="asn__label" for="asn-desc">Detail (optional)</label>
              <textarea
                class="asn__input asn__area"
                id="asn-desc"
                name="description"
                rows="3"
                maxlength="4000"
                placeholder="What done looks like, and anything they will need"
              >{assignDescription}</textarea>
            </div>

            <div class="asn__actions">
              <button class="asn__go wf-focusable" type="submit">Assign it</button>
              <p class="asn__hint">
                It lands in Assigned on their board straight away, and you become its approver.
              </p>
            </div>
          </form>
        )}

        <p class="asn__scope">
          {scopeDepartmentId
            ? 'You can give work to yourself, to anyone HR has recorded as reporting to you, and to the department you lead.'
            : 'You can give work to yourself and to anyone HR has recorded as reporting to you.'}
          {' '}
          If somebody who reports to you is missing, ask HR to set you as their reporting manager on
          their employee record.
        </p>

        {scopeDepartmentId && <p class="asn__scope">{DEPARTMENT_COVERAGE_NOTICE}</p>}

        {assignRosterTruncated && (
          <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">
            Showing the first {ASSIGN_ROSTER_LIMIT} people. There are more than this, so somebody you
            are looking for may not be in the list.
          </p>
        )}
      </div>
    </details>
  )}

  {mayUse && boardOk && (
    <div>
      <p class="tally">
        <b class="wf-num">{totalShown}</b>
        <span>{totalShown === 1 ? 'task' : 'tasks'} you can see{whoName ? ' for ' + whoName : ''}</span>
        {overdueShown > 0 && (
          <span class="tally__late" data-wf-tone="overdue">{overdueShown} past their date</span>
        )}
      </p>

      {priorityNotice && <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">{priorityNotice}</p>}
      {whoNotice && <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">{whoNotice}</p>}
      {columnNotice && <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">{columnNotice}</p>}
      {engineNotice && <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">{engineNotice}</p>}
      {truncated && (
        <p class="note" data-wf-tone="info">Showing the first {BOARD_LIMIT} tasks. There are more than this, so narrow the board with the filters below to be sure you are seeing everything.</p>
      )}

      {/* FILTERS. A GET form and no script: the result is a URL somebody can bookmark or send to a
          colleague, Back is the undo stack, and the filters survive the POST that moves a card
          because the move redirects through boardUrl(). `col` is carried so changing a filter does
          not knock you out of the single column you were reading. */}
      <FilterBar
        class="filters"
        action="/portal/tasks"
        label="Filter the board"
        submitLabel="Apply"
        clearHref={filtersOn ? '/portal/tasks' : undefined}
        clearLabel="Clear filters"
        summary={filterSummary}
        carry={oneColumn ? [{ name: 'col', value: oneColumn }] : []}
        fields={[
          { name: 'who', label: 'Person', type: 'select', value: whoValid || '', options: whoOptions },
          { name: 'prio', label: 'Priority', type: 'select', value: priority || '', options: prioOptions },
          { name: 'late', label: 'Only tasks past their date', type: 'checkbox', checked: overdueOnly },
          { name: 'arch', label: 'Include archived', type: 'checkbox', checked: includeArchived },
        ]}
      />

      {/* DENSITY AND FOCUS. Two POSTs, a cookie each, zero bytes of JavaScript. Density is the
          reason this board can be a reading surface at 09:00 and a triage queue at 17:00 without
          being two screens; focus mode drops the chrome to the quiet ink so the cards carry the
          contrast. Both are colour and spacing tokens only — nothing here re-queries anything. */}
      <form class="prefs" method="POST" action={selfUrl}>
        <input type="hidden" name="action" value="prefs"/>

        <fieldset class="prefs__set">
          <legend class="prefs__legend">Density</legend>
          <div class="prefs__row">
            {DENSITIES.map((d: string, i: number) => (
              <button
                class:list={['prefs__btn', 'wf-focusable', { 'is-on': d === density }]}
                type="submit"
                name="density"
                value={d}
                aria-pressed={d === density ? 'true' : 'false'}
              >{DENSITY_LABELS[i]}</button>
            ))}
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="prefs__set">
          <legend class="prefs__legend">Reading</legend>
          <div class="prefs__row">
            <button
              class:list={['prefs__btn', 'wf-focusable', { 'is-on': focusOn }]}
              type="submit"
              name="focus"
              value={focusOn ? 'off' : 'on'}
              aria-pressed={focusOn ? 'true' : 'false'}
            >Focus mode</button>
          </div>
        </fieldset>
      </form>

      {/* THE COLUMN CHIPS, AND THE MOBILE DECISION.
          At 360px a board of nine columns is roughly 2,400px of sideways swiping, and somebody
          hunting for their one blocked task would swipe past six columns to reach it. So the columns
          scroll horizontally INSIDE their own container — the PAGE never scrolls sideways — and
          these chips give a one-tap route to a single full-width column via ?col=. It is a real
          filter rather than a responsive trick, which is why it stays on a wide screen too: "show me
          only what is under review" is a reasonable thing to want on a laptop. The chips carry the
          counts, so the shape of the whole board is readable without scrolling at all. */}
      <nav class="chips" aria-label="Jump to one column">
        <a
          class:list={['chip', 'wf-focusable', { 'is-on': !oneColumn }]}
          href={boardUrl({ col: null })}
          aria-current={!oneColumn ? 'true' : undefined}
        >All columns <span class="chip__n wf-num">{totalShown}</span></a>
        {columns.map((c: any) => (
          <a
            class:list={['chip', 'wf-focusable', { 'is-on': oneColumn === c.status }]}
            href={boardUrl({ col: c.status })}
            aria-current={oneColumn === c.status ? 'true' : undefined}
          >{c.label} <span class="chip__n wf-num">{c.tasks.length}</span></a>
        ))}
      </nav>

      {totalShown === 0 ? (
        <EmptyState
          class="boardempty"
          headingLevel={2}
          illustration={filtersOn ? 'search' : 'tray'}
          tone={filtersOn ? 'draft' : 'approved'}
          title={filtersOn ? 'Nothing matches these filters' : 'No work is on your board'}
          body={filtersOn
            ? 'The filters are narrower than the board. Clear them to see everything you can see.'
            : 'Tasks appear here once somebody assigns work to you, or once you are added to a task as a reviewer, an approver or a watcher.'}
          actionLabel={filtersOn ? 'Clear filters' : 'Go to My HR'}
          actionHref={filtersOn ? '/portal/tasks' : '/portal/employee'}
        />
      ) : (
        <div id="board" class:list={['board', { 'is-single': !!oneColumn }]} tabindex="-1">
          {visibleColumns.map((c: any) => (
            <section class="col" data-status={c.status} aria-labelledby={'colh-' + c.status}>

              {/* THE STICKY HEADER, AND WHERE IT IS GENUINELY STICKY.
                  Multi-column: .col is capped in height and .col__body is the scroller, so the
                  header is a non-scrolling flex sibling — always visible, no sticky needed, and the
                  cap means a three-card column stays three cards tall. Single column: there is no
                  cap and no inner scroller, so position:sticky pins the header to the VIEWPORT as
                  the page scrolls. Both are real. What is NOT attempted is a viewport-sticky header
                  inside the horizontal scroller: an overflow-x:auto element is a scroll container in
                  both axes, so sticky there resolves against a box that never scrolls vertically and
                  silently does nothing.
                  z-index is 2, not --wf-z-sticky (100): the bottom tab bar is at 40, and a column
                  header at 100 would paint straight over it. */}
              <div class="colhead">
                <h2 class="colhead__name" id={'colh-' + c.status}>{c.label}</h2>
                <span class="colhead__n wf-num">{c.tasks.length}</span>
              </div>

              <div class="col__body">
                {c.tasks.length === 0 && (
                  <EmptyState
                    compact={true}
                    headingLevel={3}
                    illustration={emptyArt(c.status)}
                    tone={emptyTone(c.status)}
                    title={emptyTitle(c.status)}
                  />
                )}

                {(c.tasks as BoardTask[]).map((t: BoardTask) => (
                  <div class="tw" data-form={'mv-' + t.id}>
                    <TaskCard
                      headingLevel={3}
                      title={t.title || 'Untitled task'}
                      href={'/portal/tasks/' + t.id}
                      status={t.status}
                      statusLabel={t.statusLabel}
                      priority={t.priority}
                      dueOn={t.dueOn}
                      isOverdue={t.isOverdue}
                      assignee={{ name: t.employeeName, role: t.selfAssigned ? 'Self-assigned' : null }}
                      description={t.description}
                      blockedReason={t.blockedReason}
                      cancelReason={t.cancelReason}
                      commentCount={t.commentCount}
                      assignedByName={t.assignedByName}
                      createdAt={t.createdAt}
                      statusChangedAt={t.statusChangedAt}
                      completedAt={t.completedAt}
                      detailsLabel="Details"
                    >
                      {/* THE REAL MECHANISM. Options come from t.allowedTransitions, which the
                          engine computed from roles the database resolved — never from
                          TASK_STATUSES, which would offer moves this person cannot make. */}
                      <form slot="actions" class="mv" method="POST" action={selfUrl} id={'mv-' + t.id}>
                        <input type="hidden" name="action" value="move"/>
                        <input type="hidden" name="task_id" value={t.id}/>
                        {t.allowedTransitions.length > 0 ? (
                          <div class="mv__row">
                            {/* A REAL <label>, NOT aria-label={... + t.title + ...}.
                                A task title is text somebody typed. Astro escapes the quote in an
                                attribute, so an aria-label cannot be broken out of and this was
                                never an injection — but it leaves raw < and > sitting inside the
                                attribute, and a screen reader then reads the punctuation out to the
                                one person the label exists for. As element text it is escaped
                                unambiguously, and a labelled control is better semantics than an
                                aria-label regardless. The id is the task's own UUID, so it is
                                unique on a board of any size. */}
                            <label class="wf-sr-only" for={'to-' + t.id}>
                              {'Move ' + (t.title || 'this task') + ' to'}
                            </label>
                            <select
                              class="mv__select"
                              id={'to-' + t.id}
                              name="to"
                              required
                            >
                              <option value="">Move to</option>
                              {(t.allowedTransitions as string[]).map((s: string) => (
                                <option value={s}>{labelOf(s)}</option>
                              ))}
                            </select>
                            <button class="mv__go wf-focusable" type="submit">Move</button>
                          </div>
                        ) : (
                          <p class="mv__none">Nothing you can move this to from here.</p>
                        )}
                        {t.allowedTransitions.length > 0 && needsReasonBox(t) && (
                          <input
                            class="mv__reason"
                            type="text"
                            name="reason"
                            maxlength="2000"
                            aria-label="Why, if you are blocking or cancelling it"
                            placeholder="If blocking or cancelling, say why"
                          />
                        )}
                      </form>
                    </TaskCard>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </section>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      <p class="hint">Drag a card onto a column to move it, or use the Move button on the card. Both do the same thing, and the Move button is the one that works everywhere.</p>
    </div>
  )}
</main>

{/* THE COMPOSED BAR. This was `<BottomNav variant="tasks" active="tasks" />` — Home | Tasks | Learn |
